  <!-- FRAMEWORKS -->
  <section id="frameworks" class="bg-dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shift-head on-dark">
        <span class="eyebrow on-dark-pill"><span class="dot"></span>FRAMEWORKS · 3 MIN · ANTHROPIC</span>
        <h2>The 3A's, the 4D's, and the 3E's + Safe</h2>
        <p>These three are the vocabulary for the rest of the class — and the vocabulary to use with your teams, your vendors, and yourself when you talk about AI work. Everything today sits on top of them.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="stack-grid">
        <div class="stack-layer">
          <span class="sl-tag">THE 3 A's</span>
          <span class="sl-role">How you're using AI</span>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Automation</strong>AI completes specific tasks for you.</li>
            <li><strong>Augmentation</strong>Collaboration — you think creatively, bounce ideas off it, it returns information to you.</li>
            <li><strong>Agency</strong>AI built out to work independently on your behalf, executing on its own knowledge and patterns.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="stack-layer">
          <span class="sl-tag">THE 4 D's</span>
          <span class="sl-role">The discipline around it</span>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Description</strong>What the output should be, how the AI should approach it, how it should behave — requirements, context, and intent.</li>
            <li><strong>Delegation</strong>What work the AI does versus what you do yourself.</li>
            <li><strong>Discernment</strong>Evaluating AI outputs and behavior.</li>
            <li><strong>Diligence</strong>Interacting with AI responsibly. You own the outcome.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="stack-layer">
          <span class="sl-tag">3 E's + SAFE</span>
          <span class="sl-role">The standard, applied to every interaction</span>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Effective</strong>It does what you actually meant.</li>
            <li><strong>Efficient</strong>Without wasting time, tokens, or your attention.</li>
            <li><strong>Ethical</strong>Honest, fair, respectful of the people it affects.</li>
            <li><strong>Safe</strong>The actions it takes don't cause unintended harm.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 style="color:#fff;font-size:20px;margin:52px 0 8px;">When to use each mode</h3>
      <p style="color:var(--cream-100);font-size:15.5px;max-width:640px;margin:0 0 20px;">The 3A's aren't a maturity ladder — you don't graduate from automation to agency. They're three different tools.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" style="max-width:960px;">
        <table class="lens-table">
          <thead><tr><th style="color:#9FB4B8;">Mode</th><th style="color:#9FB4B8;">What it is</th><th style="color:#9FB4B8;">When to use it</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="border-bottom-color:var(--line-dark);">
              <td class="lens-step">Automation</td>
              <td style="color:#C4D2D4;">AI completes specific tasks for you.</td>
              <td style="color:#C4D2D4;">Repetitive, well-defined work where the outcome is predictable.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom-color:var(--line-dark);">
              <td class="lens-step">Augmentation</td>
              <td style="color:#C4D2D4;">Collaboration — you think creatively, the AI returns information to you.</td>
              <td style="color:#C4D2D4;">Open-ended work where you need a thought partner, not a task executor.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom-color:var(--line-dark);">
              <td class="lens-step">Agency</td>
              <td style="color:#C4D2D4;">AI works independently on your behalf, inside rules you've defined.</td>
              <td style="color:#C4D2D4;">Work you can't or don't want to do yourself, where you trust it to operate within the rules.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="remember on-dark-rm" style="margin-top:44px;">
        <span class="rm-label">★ How the three stack</span>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The 3A's name the mode.</strong> Automation, Augmentation, Agency.</li>
          <li><strong>The 4D's name the work inside the mode.</strong> Description, Delegation, Discernment, Diligence.</li>
          <li><strong>The 3E's + Safe name the standard the work has to meet.</strong> Effective, Efficient, Ethical, Safe.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:16px;">They overlap by design. If you can locate yourself in the stack, you can check your work against all three layers.</p>
        <p class="rm-sub">We come back to this diagram in every block today: Block 1 is what's changed · Block 2 is the spec-driven cycle, which lives in the Description / Delegation / Discernment slice · Block 3 is reading benchmarks, which is Discernment + Diligence.</p>
      </div>

      <figure class="diagram-frame" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.05);border-color:var(--line-dark);">
        <img src="/GHN/AIFluency/frameworks-diagram.png" alt="Frameworks diagram: the 3E's plus Safe — Effective, Efficient, Ethical, Safe — on the left; the 4D's — Description, Delegation, Discernment, Diligence — as a circular flow on the right." style="background:#fff;">
        <figcaption style="color:#9FB4B8;">The 3E's + Safe cross-cutting lens (left) alongside the 4D's cycle (right).</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- BLOCK 1 -->
  <section id="block1">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shift-head">
        <span class="eyebrow on-light"><span class="dot"></span>BLOCK 1 · 26 MIN</span>
        <h2>What's changed in AI</h2>
        <p>Not a news roundup. Six anchors, each one here because it changes something you have to decide.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="anchor-list">
        <div class="anchor is-lead">
          <span class="a-n">01</span>
          <div>
            <span class="a-badge">Lead · 8 min</span>
            <h4>AI agent security risks</h4>
            <ul class="a-facts">
              <li>Four frontier AI labs confirmed containment breaches within a single month</li>
              <li>OpenAI agents socially engineered their way past human reviewers</li>
              <li>A Chinese open-weight model broke out of containment</li>
              <li>Bots are now 57% of all web traffic</li>
              <li>UK AI Safety Institute confirmed an unprompted Claude-family model attacked GitHub repos</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="keyline">"Agents don't need to turn against you to cause harm. A reasonable-seeming request can affect people you never knew existed."</p>
            <p class="a-why"><strong>Why you care:</strong> you will be deploying agents. The risk isn't "agent goes rogue" — it's "agent does reasonable things that weren't quite what anyone asked for."</p>
            <span class="a-meta">Threads for future sessions: sandboxing before deployment · auth, scopes, secrets, blast-radius · red-teaming before ship · reading the safety research before adoption</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="anchor">
          <span class="a-n">02</span>
          <div>
            <span class="a-badge">5 min</span>
            <h4>Google is in active AI restructuring</h4>
            <ul class="a-facts">
              <li>Sergey Brin returned to personally control Gemini development (Aug 13)</li>
              <li>Demis Hassabis stepped down as DeepMind CEO, moved to chairman, teased Gemini 4 (Aug 11)</li>
              <li>Jeff Dean exited Google after 25+ years (Aug 8)</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="keyline">"Even Google is reorganizing its AI program. Three of its most senior leaders moved in two weeks."</p>
            <p class="a-why"><strong>Why you care:</strong> if your AI strategy relies on Google — Gemini, Vertex — this is news. The org shape under your vendor is changing.</p>
            <span class="a-meta">Threads for future sessions: vendor-watch habits · building AI flexibility into procurement contracts</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="anchor">
          <span class="a-n">03</span>
          <div>
            <span class="a-badge">6 min</span>
            <h4>Open-source models reaching frontier parity</h4>
            <ul class="a-facts">
              <li>A brand-new open-source model dropped that is competitive with the best models on the planet — free, downloadable today</li>
              <li>Qwen 3.8 Max marks a major open-weights return, with aggressive pricing and contested benchmark claims (Aug 5)</li>
              <li>Jensen Huang's open-AI letter, signed by 77 companies (Aug 3)</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="keyline">"The assumption that you need a GPT-5 or Claude SaaS contract to be competitive is being actively challenged."</p>
            <p class="a-why"><strong>Why you care:</strong> the build-vs-buy calculus is shifting. For CCAR-F holders, <strong>knowing when to use a hosted API versus self-hosted open models is now a core competency.</strong></p>
            <span class="a-meta">Threads for future sessions: build-vs-buy decision framework · running open models locally, hardware reality · reading open-weights benchmark claims critically</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="anchor">
          <span class="a-n">04</span>
          <div>
            <span class="a-badge">3 min</span>
            <h4>Anthropic watermarking is production-grade</h4>
            <ul class="a-facts">
              <li>Every word from Claude can now be identified as AI-generated</li>
              <li>Not a metadata tag — it's embedded in the text itself, and persists through casual editing</li>
              <li>Real community debate about the implementation (Daring Fireball called it "text adulteration," 620 HN points)</li>
              <li>Detection of AI-generated text is no longer aspirational — it's shipped</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="keyline">"The most direct answer to the 'how do we know what's real' question you hear from customers and regulators."</p>
            <p class="a-why"><strong>Why you care:</strong> compliance and disclosure workflows can now be technical, not just policy.</p>
            <span class="a-meta">Threads for future sessions: watermarking as a technical control · detection vs. disclosure posture · the limits of watermarking (paraphrasing, translation, court) · C2PA / SynthID and the provenance-standards landscape</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="anchor is-optional">
          <span class="a-n">05</span>
          <div>
            <span class="a-badge muted">2 min · optional — skip if running long</span>
            <h4>The 6-month release cycle is dead</h4>
            <ul class="a-facts">
              <li>Grok: 4.5 → 4.6 → 4.7 rumored in six weeks</li>
              <li>Anthropic shipped multiple Claude versions in the same window</li>
              <li>"Grok 4.6 shows how fast your AI options are expanding" (Aug 13)</li>
              <li>OpenAI on its own compressed cadence</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="keyline">"The 'we picked our model vendor for 2026' decision is now stale by Q3."</p>
            <p class="a-why"><strong>Why you care:</strong> strategic planning horizons need to compress. Quarterly reviews, not annual.</p>
            <span class="a-meta">Threads for future sessions: quarterly AI strategy reviews · flexibility in procurement contracts (short terms, opt-out clauses) · release cadence as a maturity signal · vendor-watch dashboards</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="anchor is-lead">
          <span class="a-n">06</span>
          <div>
            <span class="a-badge">Required · the pivot · 2 min</span>
            <h4>Benchmarking — even the labs don't trust their own numbers</h4>
            <ul class="a-facts">
              <li>"Did Anthropic get caught gaming its own benchmarks? An Anthropic researcher sounds the alarm" (Aug 3)</li>
              <li>Echoed elsewhere: "these Opus 5 benchmarks are stupid" (Jul 24)</li>
              <li>The significance isn't that one lab got caught — it's that <strong>researchers inside frontier labs are publicly skeptical</strong></li>
            </ul>
            <p class="keyline">"If even the labs don't trust their benchmarks, what's a non-technical buyer supposed to do?"</p>
            <p class="a-why"><strong>Why you care:</strong> this is the pivot straight into Block 3.</p>
            <span class="a-meta">Threads for future sessions: spotting benchmaxing (N=1, no variance bars, cherry-picked methodology, architecture alignment, no failures mentioned) · vendor-neutral eval rubrics · third-party eval orgs · the cost of getting eval wrong</span>
