  <!-- BLOCK 2 -->
  <section id="block2" class="bg-paper">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shift-head">
        <span class="eyebrow on-light"><span class="dot"></span>BLOCK 2 · 28 MIN · THE CORE OF THE CLASS</span>
        <h2>Spec-Driven Development</h2>
        <p>We go deep on the software case today, because that's where the cycle shows its real value — coordinating multiple actors, human and AI, against a shared reference. The discipline transfers. But it has to be earned somewhere real first.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="remember">
        <span class="rm-label">★ Why this matters</span>
        <p>"Spec-driven development solves a <strong>coordination problem</strong>. When you're shipping software with multiple agents or people, the spec is the shared reference. Without it, you're arguing about what you meant."</p>
      </div>

      <div style="max-width:880px;margin:30px 0 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size:18.5px;margin-bottom:12px;">Vocabulary, quickly</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15.5px;color:var(--slate-500);margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;"><strong style="color:var(--ink-900);">Spec</strong> — the document describing what you're building, for whom, and what success looks like. Same thing as the PRD you wrote last week.</p>
        <p style="font-size:15.5px;color:var(--slate-500);margin:0;line-height:1.6;"><strong style="color:var(--ink-900);">Spec-driven development</strong> — write the spec first, plan against it, break it into tasks, execute the tasks against the spec. Industry term: GitHub, Brian Casel's Agent OS, and AI Native DevCon all use it.</p>
      </div>

      <figure class="diagram-frame on-light" style="max-width:900px;margin:36px auto 44px;">
        <img src="/GHN/AIFluency/spec-driven-development.png" alt="Spec-Driven Development: four connected stages — Specify (define clear project requirements), Plan (outline project timeline and resources), Tasks (break down project into manageable tasks), and Implement (execute tasks and build project) — linked by a looping dashed flow.">
        <figcaption>The four steps, in order. The review loops — rewrite the spec, adversarially review the plan — live in the gaps between them.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <!-- STAGE 1 -->
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="stage-num"><span class="sn">Step 1</span><span class="st">6 min</span></div>
        <div class="stage-body">
          <h3>Specify</h3>
          <p>The muscle is the same one from Week 1: <strong style="color:var(--ink-900);">requirements, context, and intent</strong>. What goes in:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Who it's for</strong> — the user, the customer, the audience.</li>
            <li><strong>What success looks like</strong> — how do you know it worked? The metric, the behavior, the outcome.</li>
            <li><strong>What's NOT in this version</strong> — explicitly. The out-of-scope list matters as much as the in-scope list. Without it, the spec drifts.</li>
            <li><strong>Requirements</strong> — features, behaviors, edge cases. Include the minimum-viable version <em>and</em> the "we'll get to it later" version.</li>
            <li><strong>Architecture considerations</strong> — even if you're not technical, name what has to be in place up front to support later features. "If we want SSO later, the user table needs to support it from day one, so don't cut that corner now."</li>
            <li><strong>What you expect to see</strong> — the rambly part. Talk through how it might look, how it would feel to use. This is where requirements become vivid.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="remember">
            <span class="rm-label">★ Remember</span>
            <p>Be thorough. <strong>Don't assume the AI will fill in what you didn't say</strong> — it will fill in something, but probably not what you meant.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- STAGE 1.5 -->
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="stage-num"><span class="sn">Step 1.5</span><span class="st">4 min</span></div>
        <div class="stage-body">
          <h3>Write the spec — then review it</h3>
          <div class="handoff"><span class="hf-label">Handoff</span><span>"Write me a spec for this."</span></div>
          <p>Hand the AI the brain dump — requirements, context, intent, the rambly forward-looking part. It will come back better organized than what you gave it. It will also be wrong in ways that matter.</p>
          <div class="review-loop">
            <h4>Review it the way you'd review any co-worker's work</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>What's missing?</li>
              <li>What's wrong?</li>
              <li>What did the AI assume that you never said?</li>
              <li>What got cut from your forward-looking part that actually mattered?</li>
              <li>Is the out-of-scope list still accurate?</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p>Edit, update, adjust, then ask for the spec back and read it again. Usually 2–3 passes before you trust it.</p>
          <div class="remember">
            <span class="rm-label">★ Remember</span>
            <p>Spec-driven development is <strong>iterative</strong>. The first draft isn't the spec — the first draft is the conversation you needed to have with yourself, made visible.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- STAGE 2 -->
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="stage-num"><span class="sn">Step 2</span><span class="st">5 min</span></div>
        <div class="stage-body">
          <h3>Plan</h3>
          <div class="handoff"><span class="hf-label">Handoff</span><span>"Here's the spec. Build me a plan for developing it."</span></div>
          <p>What the plan has to contain:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Phasing</strong> — what gets built first, second, last. Foundation before features. Auth before sharing. Data model before UI.</li>
            <li><strong>Dependencies</strong> — if A depends on B and B depends on C, you can't ship A until C is done.</li>
            <li><strong>Restrictions and constraints</strong> — budget, timeline, what's allowed, what isn't.</li>
            <li><strong>Tech stack</strong> — languages, frameworks, services, infrastructure. Where the work runs.</li>
            <li><strong>Risks and unknowns</strong> — what's likely to bite you, what you're not sure about.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="review-loop">
            <h4>The adversarial review — the advanced move</h4>
            <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--slate-500);margin:0 0 10px;">Hand the plan to a <em>different</em> AI: "Review this plan adversarially. What did the first AI miss? What choices did it make that you disagree with? What are the failure modes?" Then use the answer three ways:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong style="color:var(--ink-900);">As a learning experience</strong> — if you don't know why a choice was made, ask. "You picked Postgres over MongoDB — why?"</li>
              <li><strong style="color:var(--ink-900);">As a back-and-forth</strong> — argue with it. Maybe the first AI was right. Maybe the second. The argument is the value.</li>
              <li><strong style="color:var(--ink-900);">As a missed-requirements check</strong> — the second AI often spots something the first dropped on the floor.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="remember">
            <span class="rm-label">★ Remember</span>
            <p>Don't skip the review. <strong>The plan is the document you'll execute against.</strong> If it's wrong, everything downstream is wrong.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- STAGE 3 -->
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="stage-num"><span class="sn">Step 3</span><span class="st">3 min</span></div>
        <div class="stage-body">
          <h3>Tasks</h3>
          <div class="handoff"><span class="hf-label">Handoff</span><span>"Break this plan into a task list. I want it phased."</span></div>
          <p>What makes a good task list:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Small enough that one agent can finish it without running out of context.</strong> If a task needs the whole codebase in its head, it's too big. Break it down.</li>
            <li><strong>Parallel work is flagged.</strong> Two tasks with no shared dependency can run at the same time.</li>
            <li><strong>Each task names the specialization it needs.</strong> "Build the auth layer" wants a security-focused context. "Design the dashboard UI" wants a design context. Different agents, different setups.</li>
            <li><strong>Phasing is reflected in the order.</strong> Phase 2 doesn't start until Phase 1 is done.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="remember">
            <span class="rm-label">★ Remember</span>
            <p>The task list is what the agents actually work on. <strong>If it's vague, the work is vague. If it's specific, the work is specific.</strong> The discipline lives at this layer.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- STAGE 4 -->
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="stage-num"><span class="sn">Step 4</span><span class="st">2 min</span></div>
        <div class="stage-body">
          <h3>Implement</h3>
          <div class="handoff"><span class="hf-label">Handoff</span><span>"Go."</span></div>
          <p>Three flavors of implementation:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>You do it.</strong> Some tasks are small enough, or important enough, that you take them yourself. The plan said so.</li>
            <li><strong>Multiple agents in parallel.</strong> Kick off everything that can run at once. Watch them. Trust but verify.</li>
            <li><strong>An orchestrator plus sub-agents.</strong> One agent holds the plan and task list, hands work to sub-agents as tasks become available, and moves to the next when they report back.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="remember">
            <span class="rm-label">★ Remember</span>
            <p>The spec stays the reference point. If a task goes off-script, you go back to the spec — <strong>the spec is the tiebreaker.</strong></p>
            <p>Implement is the shortest step in time and the most expensive in compute. The discipline you put into Specify, Plan, and Tasks is what makes it succeed or fail. <strong>A bad spec with a great agent team still produces the wrong thing.</strong></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="remember" style="margin-top:40px;">
        <span class="rm-label">★ Block 2 in one paragraph</span>
        <p>Spec-driven development is <strong>four steps and three review loops.</strong> Specify (requirements, context, intent). Plan (phasing and dependencies). Tasks (parallel work and agent specialization). Implement (against the spec). <strong>The discipline is in the iteration between the steps</strong> — review the spec, review the plan, review the tasks, then go.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- BLOCK 3 -->
  <section id="block3" class="bg-dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shift-head on-dark">
        <span class="eyebrow on-dark-pill"><span class="dot"></span>BLOCK 3 · 7 MIN</span>
        <h2>The Benchmarking Reality Check</h2>
        <p>Block 1 ended with the benchmaxing story. Here's what to actually do about it — three things to remember, and five questions to ask.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="three-things">
        <div class="tt-card">
          <span class="tt-n">★ 01</span>
          <h4>The model card is the floor</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Every model release comes with a model card — what it does, how it was trained, what it was tested on, and where it fails.</li>
            <li><strong>Always read it before you trust a vendor's claim.</strong></li>
            <li>Good model cards are honest about limitations. Bad ones hide them.</li>
            <li>Anthropic publishes one for every Claude release.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tt-card">
          <span class="tt-n">★ 02</span>
          <h4>A benchmark number is a starting point, not a verdict</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>"92.3% on MMLU" with no other context is <strong>an anecdote dressed up as data.</strong></li>
            <li>Ran once (N=1), no variance bars, only wins shown? They're curating the report.</li>
            <li>What you want: N=5 or higher, error bars, full methodology, honest failure modes.</li>
            <li>Worth knowing: MMLU (general knowledge), HumanEval (code), SWE-bench (real GitHub issues), GPQA (graduate reasoning), ARC-AGI.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tt-card">
          <span class="tt-n">★ 03</span>
          <h4>Run your own eval before you commit</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>The leaderboard tells you what a model does on <em>someone else's</em> workload. Your eval tells you what it does on <strong>yours</strong>.</li>
            <li>The minimum: 20–50 real examples from your actual use case, hand-labeled with what "good" looks like, run across 3–5 candidate models.</li>
            <li>Vendors are happy to promise benchmark scores. They're less happy to show failure modes on your workload.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="link-list" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <a class="link-pill" href="https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/about-claude/models" target="_blank" rel="noopener">↗ Anthropic model cards</a>
        <a class="link-pill" href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/open-llm-leaderboard/open_llm_leaderboard" target="_blank" rel="noopener">↗ Hugging Face Open LLM Leaderboard</a>
        <a class="link-pill" href="https://crfm.stanford.edu/helm/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">↗ Stanford HELM</a>
        <a class="link-pill" href="https://mlcommons.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">↗ MLCommons</a>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-top:64px;">
        <h3 style="color:#fff;font-size:26.5px;margin-bottom:10px;">How to compare models</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--cream-100);font-size:16px;max-width:660px;margin:0 0 30px;">When a vendor says "our model is better than X," run these five. You can use this in a vendor conversation tomorrow.</p>

        <div class="q-list">
          <div class="q-row">
            <span class="q-n">01</span>
            <div>
              <h4>Better on what benchmark?</h4>
              <p>A model better on MMLU (multiple-choice knowledge) may not be better on SWE-bench (real GitHub issues). Make them name the benchmark — then ask whether it maps to your use case.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="q-row">
            <span class="q-n">02</span>
            <div>
              <h4>Better by how much?</h4>
              <p>A 2-point difference on MMLU is noise. A 10-point difference on a relevant benchmark is signal. Don't accept "better" without a delta.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="q-row">
            <span class="q-n">03</span>
            <div>
              <h4>Compared to what version?</h4>
              <p>"Better than GPT-4" was a real claim in 2024. The same sentence in 2026 means "better than an 18-month-old model." Ask which version, from when.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="q-row">
            <span class="q-n">04</span>
            <div>
              <h4>At what cost?</h4>
              <p>Faster model, same benchmark, better for production. A 5% benchmark edge that costs 3× more per token is a bad deal for most workloads.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="q-row">
            <span class="q-n">05</span>
            <div>
              <h4>For what use case?</h4>
              <p>A model that wins on creative writing is not a model that wins on JSON extraction. Ask: "Which benchmark maps to the thing I'm actually going to deploy?"</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="remember on-dark-rm center" style="margin-top:44px;">
        <span class="rm-label">★ Block 3 in one line</span>
        <p><strong>Benchmaxing is real, and the labs are doing it.</strong> Read the model card. Look for the variance. Run your own eval before you commit.</p>
        <p class="rm-sub">The deep dive — model cards line by line, building your own eval set — is Week 4. Bring your own use case.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

